the ancient Greeks told of how  Prometheus stole fire from the gods and  brought it to humankind  today prometheus is bringing better  observability to cover nannies operators  as a leading open-source monitoring  framework in this episode we'll look at  how to set up Prometheus on ggae and use  it to instrument your application with  custom metrics this is the stack doctor  [Music]  last time we looked at how to use open  census to add custom metrics to our  application today we'll do the same with  Prometheus before we start a bit of  background prometheus is an open source  systems monitoring and alerting toolkit  originally built at SoundCloud it has a  very active developer and user community  and join the cloud native computing  foundation in 2016 so how do you set it  up the fastest and easiest way to  actually set up for me theist and your  gke cluster is to use helm which is a  package manager for kubernetes once you  have helm installed and configured you  can install Prometheus in your cluster  simply by running the helm install  stable slash Prometheus command the  install will create multiple components  the Prometheus server itself and export  are running on every node to capture  node statistics alert manager to send  email notifications and the push gateway  to accept metrics to be pushed to  Prometheus will primarily interact with  the server let's validate the  installation by port forwarding the  server pod to our localhost this allows  us to access the Prometheus UI and graph  metrics to confirm that data collection  and persistence are working now that  Prometheus is installed we are ready to  start adding metric instrumentation to  our application but first let's quickly  review the kinds of metrics that  Prometheus supports a counter is a  cumulative metric that represents a  single increasing value that can only  increase or be reset to zero and restart  for example you can use a counter to  represent a number of requests served  tasks completed or errors a gage is a  metric that represents a single  numerical value that can arbitrarily go  up and down gages are typically used for  measured values like temperatures or  current memory usage but also counts  like the number of concurrent requests a  histogram samples observation  and counts them in configurable buckets  it also provides a sum of all observed  values histograms are most often used to  measure the distributions of things like  request durations or response sizes  finally a summary samples observations  and provides a total count and a sum of  all observed values it also calculates  configurable quantiles over a sliding  time window summaries are usually used  to values where the total value is  important like payload sizes now that we  know what kind of metrics we can expose  in our application it's important to  understand how Prometheus itself works  it's a basic pull model our application  needs to expose a dedicated end point  where the metrics will be available and  we'll need to tell Prometheus where that  end point is and how frequently  described for metrics let's do both of  those now first let's instrument our  application we'll create a simple go  application expose a gauge metric in it  here's the code you can find a link to  the repo in the video description the  first step is to define the metric  itself as a gauge and provide its name  in a description next we create a  function that will set the value of our  metric in our example we're just  generating a random value so that we  know it'll change every time now we can  run our application when we access the  metrics endpoint we can see that our  custom metric is generated and shown  here now our application is instrumented  with a custom metric which is being  exported to Prometheus we need to tell  Prometheus where to find this  information we'll do that by adding some  annotations in the deployment spec in  our application before we deploy to  kubernetes will enable scraping and  define the port an end point where the  metrics are available our last step is  to set up stackdriver integration to  have this metric being just a tin to  stack driver will install the stack  driver collector on our cluster which  will patch the Prometheus server  deployment now when we go to metrics  Explorer we can see our random value  metric there I hope that you've seen how  Prometheus is a fantastic way to add  custom metric instrumentation to your  application and have those metrics being  just an intact driver thank you for  joining me today and come back next time  when we explore options for managing  monitoring and logging costs that's it  for this episode this is the stack  doctor stay healthy out there  [Music]  you  [Music]
 

